{
  "topic": "The first pulsar's pulses arrive 7.8 s later at 81.5 MHz than at high frequency, and inside each pulse the sub-pulses march across the window every few rotations.",
  "domain": "pulsar-b1919-dispersion-and-drift",
  "wildcard_chosen": true,
  "summary": "PSR B1919+21 (CP 1919), found by Jocelyn Bell Burnell in 1967, rotates every 1.337 s. Free electrons between us and the star slow low radio frequencies, so each pulse sweeps down the band with delay proportional to the inverse square of frequency; with DM 12.444 the 81.5 MHz pulse lags by about 7.8 s, several rotations. Within single pulses, sub-pulses drift steadily in longitude, forming diagonal bands when successive pulses are stacked, as in Craft's 1970 plot of 80 pulses.",
  "facts": [
    {
      "claim": "PSR B1919+21 has a period of 1.3373 s and a pulse width of 0.04 s; Jocelyn Bell Burnell discovered it on 28 November 1967, the first radio pulsar found.",
